WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.609
The next morning the rain poured down.

2
00:00:05.609 --> 00:00:10.596
When Mary looked out her window, the moor was almost hidden by gray mist.

3
00:00:10.596 --> 00:00:13.336
There would be no going out today.

4
00:00:13.336 --> 00:00:19.267
"What do you do in your cottage when it rains like this?" she asked Martha.

5
00:00:19.267 --> 00:00:23.286
"Try to stay out of each other's way," said Martha.

6
00:00:23.286 --> 00:00:29.203
"Dickon likes the wet. He says he sees things he doesn't see on fair days.

7
00:00:29.203 --> 00:00:34.740
He once found a little fox cub half-drowned in a hole after its mother died.

8
00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:38.813
He brought it home, tucked under his shirt to keep warm."

9
00:00:38.813 --> 00:00:42.606
Mary had grown to like Martha's stories.

10
00:00:42.606 --> 00:00:49.575
She especially liked the ones about Martha's mother, who seemed wise and kind, and her brother Dickon.

11
00:00:49.575 --> 00:00:53.781
"He found a crow another time," Martha went on.

12
00:00:53.781 --> 00:00:57.208
"He brought that one home too and tamed it.

13
00:00:57.208 --> 00:01:01.608
The crow's name is Soot because it's so black."

14
00:01:01.608 --> 00:01:06.203
"If I had a crow or a fox cub, I could play with it," said Mary.

15
00:01:06.203 --> 00:01:08.505
"But I have nothing."

16
00:01:08.505 --> 00:01:12.497
Martha looked puzzled. "Can you knit or sew?"

17
00:01:12.497 --> 00:01:14.122
"No."

18
00:01:14.122 --> 00:01:16.551
"Can you read?" asked Martha.

19
00:01:16.551 --> 00:01:18.545
"Yes."

20
00:01:18.545 --> 00:01:21.329
"Then why don't you read something?"

21
00:01:21.329 --> 00:01:23.837
"I don't have any books."

22
00:01:23.837 --> 00:01:31.535
"That's a pity. If Mrs. Medlock would let you go into the library, you'd have thousands of books to read."

23
00:01:31.536 --> 00:01:34.716
Mary did not care about the library.

24
00:01:34.716 --> 00:01:39.590
But hearing about it reminded her of the many rooms in the house.

25
00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:43.335
Were there really a hundred? Were they really all locked?

26
00:01:43.335 --> 00:01:48.388
Maybe she could count them. That would give her something to do.

27
00:01:48.388 --> 00:01:53.131
After Martha finished her work, Mary waited a few minutes.

28
00:01:53.131 --> 00:01:57.951
Then she opened her door and set off down the long corridor.

29
00:01:57.951 --> 00:02:05.720
It branched into other corridors, which led her up flights of steps that led to more steps.

30
00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:10.879
There were doors and more doors, and many portraits on the walls.

31
00:02:10.879 --> 00:02:16.345
The men and women wore grand costumes made of satin and velvet.

32
00:02:16.345 --> 00:02:21.125
She came upon a portrait of a plain girl rather like herself.

33
00:02:21.125 --> 00:02:26.828
"Where do you live now?" Mary asked the girl. "I wish you were here."

34
00:02:26.828 --> 00:02:30.939
It took a while before she thought to try opening a door.

35
00:02:30.939 --> 00:02:34.946
She was almost frightened when she felt the handle turn.

36
00:02:34.946 --> 00:02:42.392
She stepped into what looked like a bedroom, and on the wall was another portrait of the same little girl.

37
00:02:42.392 --> 00:02:45.005
Mary opened more doors.

38
00:02:45.005 --> 00:02:49.433
She opened so many that she grew tired of opening them. There

39
00:02:49.433 --> 00:02:53.132
could have been a hundred, though she hadn't counted them. In

40
00:02:53.132 --> 00:03:00.314
one room, which looked like a lady's sitting room, she found a cabinet full of ivory elephants. She

41
00:03:00.314 --> 00:03:03.663
played with them until she became bored. Mary

42
00:03:03.663 --> 00:03:08.127
was shutting the cabinet door when she heard a rustling sound. On

43
00:03:08.127 --> 00:03:14.264
the sofa she spotted a family of mice living in a velvet cushion. "If

44
00:03:14.264 --> 00:03:21.711
they weren't so frightened, I would take them back with me," said Mary, who was ready to return to her room. She

45
00:03:21.711 --> 00:03:25.133
went out into the corridor again. Two

46
00:03:25.133 --> 00:03:30.405
or three times she lost her way by turning down the wrong corridor. At

47
00:03:30.405 --> 00:03:37.022
last she reached her own floor, although she did not know exactly where she was. "I

48
00:03:37.022 --> 00:03:40.256
believe I've taken a wrong turn again." She

49
00:03:40.256 --> 00:03:45.232
was standing at the end of a short corridor with tapestries on the wall. "I

50
00:03:45.232 --> 00:03:49.907
don't know which way to go. How still everything is!" Suddenly

51
00:03:49.907 --> 00:03:54.247
the stillness was broken by a strange sound. It

52
00:03:54.247 --> 00:03:59.420
was a cry, but not quite like the one she'd heard before. "That

53
00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:02.804
sounds closer than it was last night." Mary's

54
00:04:02.804 --> 00:04:08.409
heart was beating faster. "And it is someone crying." She

55
00:04:08.409 --> 00:04:15.275
accidentally touched the tapestry near her and then sprang back, feeling quite startled. The

56
00:04:15.275 --> 00:04:21.214
tapestry covered a door, which swung open and revealed another corridor. Mrs.

57
00:04:21.214 --> 00:04:28.456
Medlock was coming down it with a bunch of keys in her hands and a very angry look on her face. "What

58
00:04:28.456 --> 00:04:34.669
are you doing here?" Mrs. Medlock took Mary by the arm and pulled her away. "What

59
00:04:34.669 --> 00:04:38.714
did I tell you about wandering through the house?" "I

60
00:04:38.714 --> 00:04:43.485
went the wrong way, and then I heard someone crying." "You

61
00:04:43.485 --> 00:04:47.953
heard nothing of the sort," said the housekeeper. "You

62
00:04:47.953 --> 00:04:51.660
come along back to your own rooms." And

63
00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:59.485
she half-pushed and half-pulled Mary up one corridor and down another until they were standing in Mary's rooms. "Now,"

64
00:04:59.485 --> 00:05:02.622
said Mrs. Medlock. "You

65
00:05:02.622 --> 00:05:08.360
stay where you're told to stay, or you'll find yourself locked up. Mr.

66
00:05:08.360 --> 00:05:11.866
Craven had better get you a governess. You

67
00:05:11.866 --> 00:05:16.982
need someone sharp to look after you. I've got enough to do." Mrs.

68
00:05:16.982 --> 00:05:21.936
Medlock stormed from the room and slammed the door after her. Mary

69
00:05:21.936 --> 00:05:25.184
sat on the rug before the fire. She

70
00:05:25.184 --> 00:05:29.555
did not cry, but she was pale with rage. "There

71
00:05:29.555 --> 00:05:38.439
was someone crying," she said to herself. "There was. "

